Render ONE cozy handheld pixel-art trainer reference for username "{{ username }}".

GOAL — trainer-first stylization, user-flavored wardrobe
- The result must read first as a cozy handheld monster-battler field trainer drawn in Image 2's pixel vocabulary — chunky silhouette, thick outlines, simplified sprite-scale features.
- Image 1 supplies recognizable likeness cues (hair, skin tone, ethnicity, build) and the flavor of what they wear; Image 2 supplies how to draw, not who to copy.
- Likeness means recognizable at cozy handheld sprite scale — not photo-portrait fidelity, not illustration-grade facial rendering.

REFERENCE IMAGES (attachment order)
- Image 1: User likeness — identity AND wardrobe-flavor source. Face, skin tone, ethnicity, hair, build, and the kinds/colors of clothes they wear all come from here.
- Image 2: Style-bible trainer — rendering reference ONLY (outline weight, shading steps, texture level, cozy handheld pixel density, adult-leaning proportions, face simplification). Never copy this character's face, hair, skin, body type, headwear, layers, bag, footwear, or any specific garment.

PRIORITY
- Image 2 always wins for rendering — outlines, shading, texture, density, proportions, and how much to simplify the face.
- Image 1 always wins for who this person is — face, skin tone, ethnicity, hair, and build — and for the flavor of what they wear (garment families + colors).
- The "field trainer" read always wins for overall vibe — they must look like they belong in a cozy handheld monster-battling world on a long walk. Achieve this by trainer-izing the user's real outfit, not by replacing it with a stock costume.
- If Image 1 and Image 2 disagree on identity, wardrobe, hair, or props, follow Image 1.

STYLE BIBLE (Image 2 — rendering reference only)
- Match Image 2's pixel rendering style — outline weight, shading steps, texture level, cozy handheld density, adult-leaning proportions, and face simplification level.
- Palette muting from Image 2 applies to clothing and props only — preserve Image 1's skin tone, hair color, and general facial read exactly.
- Image 2 is a rendering exemplar, not a character template. Derive the figure's identity, hair, outfit, and any props from Image 1 — read Image 2 only to learn the drawing technique.

LIKENESS LOCK (Image 1 — attached user photo)
- Identity (soft lock): the sprite must read as the same person in Image 1 at cozy handheld sprite scale — match skin tone, ethnicity, undertones, general face shape, hair color/texture/length, general build, and overall personal vibe. Stylize the face through Image 2's simplified pixel vocabulary — do NOT reproduce photo-portrait anatomy (precise nose bridge, ear detail, smile lines, or micro-facial structure). Use 2–3 flat tone skin shading; never flatten face or skin to one light tone.
- Hair (hard lock — read from Image 1 ONLY): determine hair length, silhouette, texture, parting, volume, and color strictly from the person in Image 1 (including reddish, blond, grey, or dyed tones). The style-bible trainer in Image 2 has long, wavy, shoulder-length flowing hair — that hair belongs to Image 2, is NOT the subject's hair, and must be ignored entirely. Never transfer Image 2's hair length or shape onto this person.
- Hair length clamp (hard lock): if Image 1 shows short or medium hair (cropped above the shoulders — around the ears, jaw, or neck), render it short or medium. Do NOT draw shoulder-length, long, past-the-shoulder, or flowing hair unless Image 1 unmistakably shows it. When uncertain about length, render the hair SHORTER, not longer. Only add headwear if Image 1 shows headwear.
- Ethnicity lock (hard lock): match the primary subject's apparent racial and ethnic presentation from Image 1 exactly — skin depth, undertones, melanin level, and facial structure must read as the same demographic group as the photo. If Image 1 shows a white subject, render a white subject. If Image 1 shows a Black subject, render a Black subject. If Image 1 shows a Hispanic/Latina subject, render that presentation. Never substitute a different ethnicity, never default to an ambiguous tan or brown "generic game protagonist" skin tone, and never lighten, darken, or neutralize the subject to match Image 2 or a studio-average palette.
- Group photos: when multiple people are visible, render only the primary subject (usually centered or largest adult). Apply the locks to that subject only. Ignore other faces, children, and background people.
- Framing: if the likeness photo is cropped or face-heavy, infer natural adult proportions from the person's apparent build and age — do not inflate the head from how large the face fills the crop.
- Do NOT copy the photo's background, lighting setup, logos, busy patterns, brand marks, or photographic texture.

TRAINER ARCHETYPE
- Image 1 likeness always wins for identity: face, skin tone, ethnicity, racial presentation, hair, and build — never from Image 2. Do not genericize ethnicity into a default tan protagonist.
- Wardrobe comes from the WARDROBE section below — trainer-ized from Image 1's actual clothes, not Image 2's costume and not a stock default kit.
- This person must read as a cozy handheld monster-battler field trainer on a long walk — warm, approachable, mid-century cartridge-era adventuring energy. Never render photo-real street clothes, business suits, or red-carpet formality.
- Borrow from Image 2 rendering density, outline/shading style, and face simplification — not costume, hair, face identity, or props.
- Proportions: adult-leaning silhouette from Image 1's apparent build and age — natural head-to-body ratio, not a chibi or child body. Sprite-scale readability may use a slightly large head, but torso and legs stay adult length.
- Default pose: relaxed field-trainer stance, three-quarter view facing slightly right, full body readable at cozy handheld sprite scale.
- Match rendering technique from Image 2 (outlines, shading, palette muting on garments, silhouette weight) — do NOT copy Image 2's character design.

WARDROBE — trainer-ize the user's actual outfit
- Read what the person is actually wearing in Image 1 — garment families (tee, hoodie, jacket, shirt, pants, shorts, skirt, dress, shoes), their cut, and their dominant colors — and rebuild that same outfit in the cozy handheld trainer style.
- Preserve garment families and color relationships from Image 1. A graphic tee stays a tee (clean it up, drop the logo/print into a simple motif or solid). Cargo/tactical pants stay cargo pants. A hoodie stays a hoodie. Sneakers become trail sneakers. Keep the same broad color story (e.g. dark pants stay dark, an orange top stays orange).
- Do NOT add layers, headwear, bags, or props the photo does not show. If Image 1 has no hat, draw no hat. If Image 1 has no jacket, do not add one over a tee. If Image 1 has no backpack, do not add one.
- Trainer-izing means: simplify to a clean pixel silhouette, strip real-world logos/brands/text, mute photo-real fabrics into the locked palette, and add only light field-ready polish (sturdier shoes, practical pockets) — without changing the garment type or color story.
- Formal-wear translation (only when needed): if Image 1 shows suits, blazers, dress shirts, neckties, gowns, heels, or uniforms, translate those formal items into cozy field-trainer equivalents while keeping the photo's colors — suit jacket → utility jacket or windbreaker, dress shirt → henley or tee, gown/skirt → trail skirt, heels → boots. Apply this ONLY to formal garments; leave casual garments as their own family.
- Props: only render props the photo shows (and trainer-ize them — a real phone becomes a closed clamshell field device, etc.). Do not invent belt pouches, devices, or bags that aren't in the photo.

COZY HANDHELD TRAINER RENDERING
- Full-body field trainer readable at cozy handheld sprite scale — chunky silhouette, adult-leaning proportions, warm and approachable.
- Thick #3D2B1F Tobacco Brown pixelated outlines with pixel-rounded forms. Keep silhouettes simple; avoid micro-detail.
- Shading: 2–3 flat tone steps per color zone with soft stepped edges — face, skin, hair, and garments included; preserve the photo's warm/cool undertones in skin tones. Not airbrushed gradients, not painterly blending, not illustration-grade rendering, not heavy crosshatch.
- Texture: whisper-level only. Light paper grain plus subtle dither on large flat color fills. Do NOT fill entire surfaces with stipple or crosshatch. Do NOT bake film grain, vignette, or screen-space effects into the sprite.
- Locked mid-century muted palette (~16–24 colors) for clothing and props. Skin tone and hair color follow the likeness photo — do not lighten, darken, or recolor them to match the style bible. No neon saturation, no glossy plastic, no pure black.

DO NOT RENDER
- High-resolution concept art, HD-2D painterly sprites, or illustration-scale detail
- Photo-portrait likeness, hyper-detailed facial anatomy, or realistic skin pore/line rendering
- Heavy dither crosshatch, stipple noise filling color fields, or crunchy over-texturing
- Smooth airbrushed cel shading, mascot polish, Sugimori official art, or anime key art
- Drop shadows, glow, bloom, or gradients baked into the sprite
- Photo-real street clothes, photographic texture, real-world logos, brand marks, or text

CANVAS RULES
- Background (technical chroma key): fill every non-trainer pixel with exactly {{ background_color }} at full saturation — one uniform wash, zero hue shift. This color is pre-selected to be distant from the trainer's skin, hair, and clothing; render it precisely so post-processing can remove it cleanly.
- The background must stay perfectly flat — no paper texture, grain, vignette, dither, or palette muting on the background. Style-bible muting applies to the trainer figure only.
- Paper texture and grain apply to the trainer figure only.
- Draw exactly one full-body trainer figure with margin on all sides.

FINAL CHECK
- Reads first as a cozy handheld field trainer in Image 2's chunky pixel style — then as a recognizable version of the person in Image 1.
- Same ethnicity and racial presentation, skin tone, undertones, general face shape, hair color/texture/length, and build as Image 1 — stylized, not photo-real.
- Hair length and silhouette match Image 1, NOT Image 2 — no shoulder-length, long, or flowing hair unless the photo clearly shows it.
- Wearing a trainer-ized version of Image 1's OWN outfit — same garment families and color story — not Image 2's clothing, not invented hats/jackets/bags the photo lacks, not photo-real street clothes, not business/formal wear.
- Polished mid-detail throughout: full 2–3 tone shading on skin, hair, and garments; fold/seam reads; whisper-level paper grain — never a flat single-tone face or washed-out skin.
- Background pixels must be exactly {{ background_color }} — uniform, full saturation, no muting.
